# Build a Star-Harvesting Swarm

Begin with the geometric idea: isotropic luminosity crosses every centered sphere, so full projected coverage captures the full output. Explore the linear law directly:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The companion numerical and graphical views expose the same cancellation from two angles.

{{demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the geometric theorem and assumptions</summary>

For radius $R>0$, sphere area is $S=4\pi R^2$, flux is $F=L/S$, and power captured by nonoverlapping projected area $A$ is $P=FA$. Therefore $A=S$ implies $P=L$, while $0\le A\le S$ implies $P\le L$. The model assumes isotropic radiation, common radius, and no occlusion.

[Background on the inverse-square law](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverse-square_law)
</details>

## Divide the thermal responsibility

Collection is linear, but the quadratic concentration metric is convex. Use the laboratory to subdivide a fixed total area.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the optimality proof</summary>

For areas $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ totaling $A$, Cauchy--Schwarz gives $A^2\le n\sum_i a_i^2$. Hence the load is at least $A^2/n$, attained by $a_i=A/n$. Equivalently, the gap is $\sum_i(a_i-A/n)^2$, so equality requires a uniform allocation.

[Explore convex functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convex_function) and [Cauchy--Schwarz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cauchy%E2%80%93Schwarz_inequality).
</details>

## Convert harvested energy into computation

A capacity claim is meaningful only after an energy cost per event is supplied.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the resource-accounting argument</summary>

If each event costs $c>0$ joules and $Nc\le E$, division by $c$ gives $N\le E/c$. Thus $10^{26}$ joules per second supports $10^{40}$ operations per second whenever the charged cost is at most $10^{-14}$ joules per operation. A $10^{50}$-bit statement similarly requires $c_b\le E/10^{50}$.

[Background on Landauer's principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landauer%27s_principle)
</details>

## Synthesis

Run the complete end-to-end example to compare one astronomical unit, uniform thermal allocations, and conditional computation capacities.

{{demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

The framework is intentionally modular: geometry determines captured power, convex allocation determines the stated thermal metric, and positive energy costs determine event capacities. Richer models can add occlusion, orbital dynamics, temperatures, and finite duration without confusing these layers.
